WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.208
SPEAKER 1: We got a brief
overview of service objects

00:00:02.208 --> 00:00:02.910
last season.

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.620
But it's time to take a
deeper dive into services.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:06.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:07.260
Hiya, Kaslin.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:08.160
It's good to see you.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.410
I was just getting ready to
learn more about services.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.360
Maybe you could help me out?

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:13.260
SPEAKER 2: Sure.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.900
I remember how we
covered how services

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.812
were used to abstract away pod
lifecycle events last season.

00:00:19.812 --> 00:00:20.520
SPEAKER 1: Right.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.020
Because pods are
made to be started,

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.640
stopped, terminated,
and created.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.082
But oftentimes,
applications need

00:00:25.082 --> 00:00:26.790
to have a long-lived
or permanent address

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.900
to reach for interacting
with services.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:30.750
SPEAKER 2: Yes.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:34.140
So if you had a server back
end for storing images,

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.840
it might not matter which
pod serves the data back

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:37.980
to your front end.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:39.630
But the front end
would always need

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:42.580
to know where to find your
back end to request pictures.

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:45.180
SPEAKER 1: And since each pod
gets its own IP address, that

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:47.760
would be a lot to manage,
storing an IP address whenever

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.380
a pod gets started or stopped.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:51.930
So services exist to
store them for us,

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.810
making app development much
easier and more scalable.

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:57.150
But I remember we created
a special type of service

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.610
last time called a LoadBalancer.

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:01.975
What was that about?

00:01:01.975 --> 00:01:03.600
SPEAKER 2: Well, see,
there's more than

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.670
just one type of service.

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:07.720
Depending on what
you're trying to do,

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.210
one may be more
suited to your needs.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:13.665
But before that, let's go
into how to define services.

00:01:13.665 --> 00:01:15.040
SPEAKER 1: If I
had a set of pods

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.970
that each listen
on TCP port 9376

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:20.820
and carry a label
app equals MyApp,

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:24.825
the YAML manifest file
would look like this.

00:01:24.825 --> 00:01:27.290
This specification creates
a new service object

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:30.200
called my-service,
which targets TCP port

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:35.060
9376 on any pod with the
app equals MyApp label.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:37.400
This is taking
traffic from port 9376

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:40.880
and routing it to the pod's
default port, port 80, which

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.730
greatly simplifies
app development.

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:44.840
Wait a minute.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.360
What IP address
does a service have?

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.420
When I say it's getting
traffic from port 9376, that

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:54.300
must mean the service itself
gets an IP address, right?

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:55.460
SPEAKER 2: Right.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:58.200
Kubernetes assigns this
service an IP address,

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.800
which is sometimes
called the ClusterIP.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.540
And this brings us to the
first type of service,

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.600
the default ClusterIP type.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.640
The ClusterIP
service type is only

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:10.789
reachable from other
points within the cluster.

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:13.070
We actually used these
before, last season,

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:15.260
to connect our front-end
service to the auth

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:17.240
and back-end services.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.520
All of those were
running in Kubernetes.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:22.560
So they just needed a cluster
IP to talk to each other.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.190
SPEAKER 1: So then the next type
must be the LoadBalancer type

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:27.920
that we also used
in the last episode.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:30.500
SPEAKER 2: Well, actually, no.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:34.010
See, when you create a
LoadBalancer-type service,

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:37.700
you can see that it actually has
an internal ClusterIP address.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.710
The LoadBalancer
service type also

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:43.380
uses what's called a NodePort
service underneath the hood.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:44.870
SPEAKER 1: The
documentation says

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.960
a NodePort service exposes
a service on each node's

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:49.910
IP at a static point.

00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:52.460
This type of service
also has a ClusterIP.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:57.190
And to access it, you reference
the node IP NodePort address.

00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:01.040
Um, please help me make sense
of why we'd want to do this?

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:02.510
SPEAKER 2: Great question.

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:04.640
You see, the LoadBalancer
type actually

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:06.830
uses NodePorts under the hood.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:09.890
At some level, pods
are running on nodes.

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:11.780
So the only way
to access them is

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:14.540
by actually talking to
the machine they're on.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:16.970
The difference is that
the LoadBalancer types

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:19.520
are pre-configured to use
a Cloud provider's load

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.030
balancer for you.

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:23.900
So if you're using
GKE on Google Cloud,

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.330
you'll be using Google
Cloud's load balancer

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:30.720
to distribute requests from your
service to your pod instances.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.870
But what happens if
you're running on-prem,

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:35.420
and don't have a
Cloud-provided load balancer?

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:39.090
Or what about when you want to
roll your own load balancer?

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:39.680
SPEAKER 1: Oh.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:43.650
In that case, I'd need to access
the NodePort myself, I guess.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.650
SPEAKER 2: Exactly.

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:49.580
So we have ClusterIP services
for routing internal traffic,

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:53.060
NodePorts for allowing external
access to a specific port

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:56.000
on a specific machine,
and load balancers

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.300
for using Cloud-provided
LoadBalancer to route traffic

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.867
between the service's Pods.

00:04:01.867 --> 00:04:03.200
SPEAKER 1: That's pretty robust.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:04.852
Are there any other types?

00:04:04.852 --> 00:04:07.310
SPEAKER 2: Well, there are more
advanced types of services,

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:10.670
like external name services
or headless services.

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:12.410
And there's even a
higher-level object

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:14.330
called ingress
objects that are used

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:16.519
to expose multiple services.

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:18.620
But I think we can save
those for another day.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:20.329
SPEAKER 1: Many
thanks to Kaslin again

00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:22.760
for continuing to drop
Kubernetes knowledge my way.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:24.740
I'm excited to go play
with services more

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:26.570
and see what I can
create for myself.

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:28.220
For more information
about services,

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:31.540
please check out the
links in the description.

